  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Famine Response in Zamzam ,Abu Shok and Al Salam camps</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ltpgtltbrgtAfter more than 15 months of war in Sudan, a catastrophic combination of conflict, displacement and humanitarian access constraints has resulted in famine in a camp sheltering hundreds of thousands of displaced people in Sudan&#x2019;s North Darfur Region. The Famine Review Committee (FRC)&#x2019;s conclusion that there is famine in Zamzam camp, is the first determination of famine by the Committee in more than 7 years, and only the third time a famine determination has been made since the monitoring system was created 20 years ago. The FRC warns that other parts of Sudan risk famine if concerted action is not taken. ltbrgtThe proposed project aims to reduce the risk of famine to vulnerable populations affected by displacement in Zamzam, Abu Shok and Al Salam camps.nbsp To enable targeted households (HHs) to have improved access to food, NRC will distribute through the national partner in-kind food baskets to 10,680 HHs (53,400 individuals). Each household will receive a 70% one-off ration of essential food items, including 50 kgs wheat flour, 6 kgs lentils, 3 litres cooking oi and 1 kg sugar, for a period of two months. NRC will procure the food items from markets within or closer to the locality. Food distribution will be done through our national partner World Trust Humanitarian Organisation (WTHO).nbsp The project will prioritize households identified as vulnerable based on factors such as disability status, female-headed and child-headed households and elderly status.ltbrgtlt/pgt</narrative>
  </description>
